<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">loot</ansi>

Battle spoils go into company cargo, with gold in the treasury.

  loot
  Collect eligible loot from corpses in your room, up to company capacity.
  Items that are too heavy remain. Gold does not use carrying capacity.

  loot own
  Collect only corpses claimed by your company.

  loot [corpse]
  Collect from one named corpse. get all [corpse] uses the same checks.

  autoloot on | autoloot off
  Optional automatic pickup from your claimed spoils after your battle ends.
  It starts off and never picks up another company's spoils automatically.
  If you cannot carry an item, it remains for manual pickup or more capacity.

Spoils are reserved for the winning company for 2 game hours, open to everyone
for 2 more game hours, then gone. Allies keep the rotating claim; outside one
alliance, the company doing the most damage wins it (ties: lowest player id).

Foes in zones with drop tables roll their own gear, a won random encounter
adds a cache, and a boss rolls much more (<ansi fg="command">help encounters</ansi>). Each allied company
rolls for itself: its roll is in a corpse claimed by that company, on the
same 2-and-2 game hour clock. Your battle summary lists what your company
took.

No pickup or item use during battle. Loot contains actual drops, not gear that
stays with the body. Your allies' possessions and quest items retain the normal
ownership and item rules. A restart does not preserve runtime corpses.

Gear can roll with a rarity, quality and affixes, and Rare and better is
found unidentified until a Scribe reads it: <ansi fg="command">help rarity</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help quality</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help itemlevel</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help affixes</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help identify</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help scribe</ansi>.

Gear also comes in families and tiers (<ansi fg="command">help equipmenttiers</ansi>), and goods are things
you carry to sell (<ansi fg="command">help goods</ansi>). Merchants buy gear (<ansi fg="command">help sell</ansi>),
smiths salvage it (<ansi fg="command">help salvage</ansi>), and <ansi fg="command">mark [item] junk</ansi> then <ansi fg="command">sell junk</ansi> clears what
you do not want (<ansi fg="command">help mark</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help encounters</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help treasury</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help party</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
